---

📌 Phase 1 – Planning

Purpose

Planning is the first stage of SDLC.

The team discusses:

- What problem needs to be solved?
- Is this project feasible?
- How much time will it take?
- What is the estimated budget?
- Which technologies will be used?
- How many developers are required?

Example

Suppose you want to build a School Management App.

During planning, the team decides:

- Flutter or Native?
- Firebase or MySQL?
- Android only or Android + iOS?
- Expected completion time
- Team size

Without proper planning, projects often fail.

---

📌 Phase 2 – Requirement Analysis

This is one of the most important phases.

Developers communicate with the client to understand exactly what the software should do.

Example Requirements

- Student Login
- Teacher Login
- Attendance
- Homework
- Fee Management
- Notices
- Results

If the requirements are misunderstood, the entire project may need to be rebuilt.

---

📌 Phase 3 – System Design

After understanding the requirements, the team creates the software blueprint.

This phase answers questions like:

- How many screens will the app have?
- Which database will be used?
- How will APIs work?
- What architecture will be followed?
- How will users navigate through the app?

This phase focuses on designing before coding.

---

📌 Phase 4 – Development

This is the coding phase.

Developers convert the design into a working application.

Tasks include:

- Writing code
- Building UI
- Connecting APIs
- Managing databases
- Implementing business logic

This is the phase most beginners think is the entire software development process, but it is only one part of SDLC.

---

📌 Phase 5 – Testing

Once development is complete, the software is tested carefully.

The testing team checks:

- Bugs
- Crashes
- Incorrect calculations
- Performance issues
- Security problems

The goal is to ensure the software works correctly before users receive it.

---

📌 Phase 6 – Deployment

Deployment means releasing the software for users.

Examples include:

- Publishing an Android app on Google Play Store
- Publishing an iOS app on the App Store
- Deploying a website to a server

Now users can access the software.

---

📌 Phase 7 – Maintenance

Software development does not end after deployment.

Maintenance includes:

- Fixing bugs
- Adding new features
- Improving performance
- Updating security
- Supporting new devices

Example

WhatsApp regularly releases updates with bug fixes and new features.

That is the Maintenance phase.

